{:edb/db-before-t 1674 :edb/db-after-t 1675 :edb/tx-data [[13194139534987 50 #inst "2026-10-08T13:24:02.439Z" 13194139534987 true] [17592186171751 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14045300/assets/d59da4d56d2d664641b5b52136db98c70c6d8ca3efb6903cc7524f748776486e.png)\n\n \n\nWhich of the following equations could have generated the slope field shown above?\n\n- $y' = x + y + 1$\n\n- $y' = \\dfrac{xy}{y+1}$\n\n- $y' = \\dfrac{y}{x+1}$" 13194139534987 true] [17592186171751 1100 "Which of the following equations could have generated the slope field shown above?\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3350/Source/Images/42556eddb08beba40a46abcc4af06f69.png)\n\n1. $y' = x + y + 1$\n2. $y' = \\dfrac{xy}{y+1}$\n3. $y' = \\dfrac{y}{x+1}$" 13194139534987 false] [17592186171751 1145 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14045300/assets/a692d04d777a7770bfed51d27e53e4d5a0f19860a22afa598875b64bdd85a25c.png)\n\n \n\nNotice the following properties of the given slope field:\n\n- The slopes of the slope field become vertical as $x \\to -1.$\n\n- The slopes of the slope field become horizontal at $y=0.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each equation.\n\n- Equation I does *not* give the required behavior. The right-hand side of $y' = x + y + 1$ does not evaluate to zero on $y=0$ (the value depends on $x$).\n\n- Equation II does *not* give the required behavior. The right-hand side of $y' = \\dfrac{xy}{y+1}$ evaluates to zero on $y=0,$ but \n\n$$\ny' = \\dfrac{xy}{y+1} \\to -\\dfrac{y}{y+1} \n\\quad \\textrm{as} \\quad \nx \\to -1,\n$$\n\nand hence $y'$ depends on $y$ along the line $x=-1.$\n\n- Equation III gives the required behavior. The right-hand side of $y' = \\dfrac{y}{x+1}$ evaluates to zero on $y=0,$ and \n\n$$\ny' = \\bigg| \\dfrac{y}{x+1} \\bigg| \\to \\infty\n\\quad \\textrm{as} \\quad \nx \\to -1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\"" 13194139534987 true] [17592186171751 1145 "![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3350/Source/Images/c1cf6111a0a453b8105ef790e0748df2.png)\n\nNotice the following properties of the given slope field:\n\n- The slopes of the slope field become vertical as $x \\to -1$.\n- The slopes of the slope field become horizontal at $y=0$.\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each equation.\n\n- Equation I does *not* give the required behavior. The right-hand side of $y' = x + y + 1$ does not evaluate to zero on $y=0$ (the value depends on $x$).\n- Equation II does *not* give the required behavior. The right-hand side of $y' = \\dfrac{xy}{y+1}$ evaluates to zero on $y=0$, but\n$y' = \\dfrac{xy}{y+1} \\to -\\dfrac{y}{y+1} \\quad \\textrm{as} \\quad x \\to -1$,\nand hence $y'$ depends on $y$ along the line $x=-1$.\n- Equation III gives the required behavior. The right-hand side of $y' = \\dfrac{y}{x+1}$ evaluates to zero on $y=0$, and\n$y' = \\bigg \\mid \\dfrac{y}{x+1} \\bigg \\mid \\to \\infty \\quad \\textrm{as} \\quad x \\to -1$.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\"" 13194139534987 false] [17592186171752 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14045300/assets/3a0ec9ab00e65eb1b63a6b73c81c615d7388617814ba6351b1575a96b732eb4d.png)\n\nThe diagram shown above is the slope field for which of the following differential equations?\n\n- $y' = x^2(y+1)+2(y+1)$\n\n- $y' = x(y+1)+2(y+1)$\n\n- $y' = x(y+1)^2+2(y+1)^2$" 13194139534987 true] [17592186171752 1100 "The diagram shown above is the slope field for which of the following differential equations?\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3350/Source/Images/321b6eb66ca267d3844c0960f615026d.png)\n\n1. $y' = x^2(y+1)+2(y+1)$\n2. $y' = x(y+1)+2(y+1)$\n3. $y' = x(y+1)^2+2(y+1)^2$" 13194139534987 false] [17592186171752 1145 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14045300/assets/b64005397add7c39b60c49bc708f3ee8cccc2e03786278df22e914fc928998db.png)\n\nNotice the following properties of the given slope field:\n\n- First, the slopes of the slope field are horizontal along the lines $x=-2$ and $y=-1,$ as shown above. Therefore, the right-hand side of the differential equation should evaluate to zero on both of these lines.\n\n- Second, the lines $x=-2$ and $y=-1$ divide the plane into $4$ regions. The sign of the slopes in each of the $4$ regions are easily deduced by considering the behavior of the solution curves in these regions. This information is summarized in the following table:\n\n| $y > -1$ | $\\searrow$\n\n$({\\color{red}{-}})$ | $\\nearrow$\n\n$({\\color{blue}{+}})$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $y <      -1$ | $\\nearrow$\n\n$({\\color{blue}{+}})$ | $\\searrow$\n\n$({\\color{red}{-}})$ |\n|  | $x< -2$ | $x> -2$ |\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each equation.\n\n- Equation I does *not* give the required behavior. The right-hand side of\n\n$$\ny' = x^2(y+1)+2(y+1) = (x^2 + 2)(y + 1)\n$$\n\nevaluates to zero at $y = -1$ only.\n\n- Equation II gives the required behavior. The right-hand side of\n\n$$\ny' = x(y+1)+2(y+1) = (x+2)(y+1)\n$$\n\nevaluates to zero at $x = -2$ and $y = -1,$ and the sign of the slopes in the required regions (summarized below) correspond to the given slope field.\n\n| $y > -1$ | ${\\color{red}{-}}$ | ${\\color{blue}{+}}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $y <      -1$ | ${\\color{blue}{+}}$ | ${\\color{red}{-}}$ |\n|  | $x< -2$ | $x> -2$ |\n\n- Equation III does *not* give the required behavior. The right-hand side of\n\n$$\ny' = x(y+1)^2+2(y+1)^2 = (x+2)(y+1)^2\n$$\n\nevaluates to zero at $x=-2$ and $y=-1,$ but the sign of the slopes in the required regions (summarized below) do not correspond to the given slope field.\n\n| $y >    -1$ | ${\\color{red}{-}}$ | ${\\color{blue}{+}}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $y <   -1$ | ${\\color{red}{-}}$ | ${\\color{blue}{+}}$ |\n|  | $x< -2$ | $x> -2$ |\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is equation II." 13194139534987 true] [17592186171752 1145 "![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3350/Source/Images/e8dd09383c592fafe22b0fd1c05a391a.png)\n\nNotice the following properties of the given slope field:\n\n- First, the slopes of the slope field are horizontal along the lines $x=-2$ and $y=-1$, as shown above. Therefore, the right-hand side of the differential equation should evaluate to zero on both of these lines.\n- Second, the lines $x=-2$ and $y=-1$ divide the plane into $4$ regions. The sign of the slopes in each of the $4$ regions are easily deduced by considering the behavior of the solution curves in these regions. This information is summarized in the following table:$y > -1$$\\searrow$\n\n$({\\color{red}{-}})$$\\nearrow$\n\n$({\\color{blue}{+}})$$y < -1$$\\nearrow$\n\n$({\\color{blue}{+}})$$\\searrow$\n\n$({\\color{red}{-}})$$x< -2$ $x> -2$\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each equation.\n\n- Equation I does *not* give the required behavior. The right-hand side of\n$y' = x^2(y+1)+2(y+1) = (x^2 + 2)(y + 1)$\nevaluates to zero at $y = -1$ only.\n- Equation II gives the required behavior. The right-hand side of\n$y' = x(y+1)+2(y+1) = (x+2)(y+1)$\nevaluates to zero at $x = -2$ and $y = -1$, and the sign of the slopes in the required regions (summarized below) correspond to the given slope field.$y > -1$${\\color{red}{-}}$${\\color{blue}{+}}$$y < -1$${\\color{blue}{+}}$${\\color{red}{-}}$$x< -2$ $x> -2$\n- Equation III does *not* give the required behavior. The right-hand side of\n$y' = x(y+1)^2+2(y+1)^2 = (x+2)(y+1)^2$\nevaluates to zero at $x=-2$ and $y=-1$, but the sign of the slopes in the required regions (summarized below) do not correspond to the given slope field.$y > -1$${\\color{red}{-}}$${\\color{blue}{+}}$$y < -1$${\\color{red}{-}}$${\\color{blue}{+}}$$x< -2$ $x> -2$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is equation II." 13194139534987 false] [17592186171753 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14045300/assets/fc130ae2d3404808ba1cbaa66667979728c535320934bb0dbff463ef467572e4.png)\n\nWhich of the following equations could have generated the slope field shown above?\n\n- $y' = 2xy$\n\n- $y' = -x(y-1)^2$\n\n- $y' = \\dfrac{1}{2}x^2(y-1)$" 13194139534987 true] [17592186171753 1100 "Which of the following equations could have generated the slope field shown above?\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3350/Source/Images/77e64d6b091c27df883b55b93e628c77.png)\n\n1. $y' = 2xy$\n2. $y' = -x(y-1)^2$\n3. $y' = \\dfrac{1}{2}x^2(y-1)$" 13194139534987 false] [17592186171753 1145 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14045300/assets/0c3b7cc8b39735429b1f39e8421fd280e4687f15c5806af0b102900b5e3f8cd8.png)\n\nNotice the following properties of the given slope field:\n\n- First, the slopes of the slope field are horizontal along the lines $x=0$ and $y=1,$ as shown above. Therefore, the right-hand side of the differential equation should evaluate to zero on both of these lines.\n\n- Second, the lines $x=0$ and $y=1$ divide the plane into $4$ regions. The sign of the slopes in each of the $4$ regions are easily deduced by considering the behavior of the solution curves in these regions. This information is summarized in the following table:\n\n| $y > 1$ | $\\nearrow$\n\n$({\\color{blue}{+}})$ | $\\nearrow$\n\n$({\\color{blue}{+}})$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $y <     1$ | $\\searrow$\n\n$({\\color{red}{-}})$ | $\\searrow$\n\n$({\\color{red}{-}})$ |\n|  | $x<     0$ | $x> 0$ |\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each equation.\n\n- Equation I does *not* give the required behavior. The right-hand side of $y' = 2xy$ evaluates to zero at $x=0$ and $y=0.$\n\n- Equation II does *not* give the required behavior. The right-hand side of $y' = -x(y-1)^2$ evaluates to zero at $x=0$ and $y=1,$ but the sign of the slopes in the required regions (summarized below) do not correspond to the given slope field.\n\n| $y > 1$ | ${\\color{blue}{+}}$ | ${\\color{red}{-}}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $y <     1$ | ${\\color{blue}{+}}$ | ${\\color{red}{-}}$ |\n|  | $x<     0$ | $x> 0$ |\n\n- Equation III gives the required behavior. The right-hand side of $y' = \\dfrac{1}{2}x^2(y-1)$ evaluates to zero at $x=0$ and $y=1,$ and the sign of the slopes in the required regions (summarized below) correspond to the given slope field.\n\n| $y > 1$ | ${\\color{blue}{+}}$ | ${\\color{blue}{+}}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $y <     1$ | ${\\color{red}{-}}$ | ${\\color{red}{-}}$ |\n|  | $x<     0$ | $x> 0$ |\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\"" 13194139534987 true] [17592186171753 1145 "![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3350/Source/Images/b06ebab58bfb746f35ecba54930526a3.png)\n\nNotice the following properties of the given slope field:\n\n- First, the slopes of the slope field are horizontal along the lines $x=0$ and $y=1$, as shown above. Therefore, the right-hand side of the differential equation should evaluate to zero on both of these lines.\n- Second, the lines $x=0$ and $y=1$ divide the plane into $4$ regions. The sign of the slopes in each of the $4$ regions are easily deduced by considering the behavior of the solution curves in these regions. This information is summarized in the following table:$y > 1$$\\nearrow$\n\n$({\\color{blue}{+}})$$\\nearrow$\n\n$({\\color{blue}{+}})$ $y < 1$$\\searrow$\n\n$({\\color{red}{-}})$ $\\searrow$\n\n$({\\color{red}{-}})$$x< 0$ $x> 0$\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each equation.\n\n- Equation I does *not* give the required behavior. The right-hand side of $y' = 2xy$ evaluates to zero at $x=0$ and $y=0$.\n- Equation II does *not* give the required behavior. The right-hand side of $y' = -x(y-1)^2$ evaluates to zero at $x=0$ and $y=1$, but the sign of the slopes in the required regions (summarized below) do not correspond to the given slope field.$y > 1$${\\color{blue}{+}}$${\\color{red}{-}}$ $y < 1$${\\color{blue}{+}}$ ${\\color{red}{-}}$$x< 0$ $x> 0$\n- Equation III gives the required behavior. The right-hand side of $y' = \\dfrac{1}{2}x^2(y-1)$ evaluates to zero at $x=0$ and $y=1$, and the sign of the slopes in the required regions (summarized below) correspond to the given slope field.$y > 1$${\\color{blue}{+}}$${\\color{blue}{+}}$ $y < 1$${\\color{red}{-}}$ ${\\color{red}{-}}$$x< 0$ $x> 0$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\"" 13194139534987 false] [17592186171820 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14045300/assets/4adbd41200bb3fdcd75f7f19530ca810cb67970b732e897845bfd621fee180b3.png)\n\n \n\nThe differential equation that generates the slope field shown above is given by $y' = (x-a)(y-b).$ Find the value of $a + b.$" 13194139534987 true] [17592186171820 1100 "The differential equation that generates the slope field shown above is given by $y' = (x-a)(y-b)$. Find the value of $a + b$.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3350/Source/Images/fd5112fd081ff4dbdf41ceb7d6b6ccc4.png)" 13194139534987 false] [17592186171820 1145 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14045300/assets/5401c18589eeafd8ac4139de98fb41879d03c0731938c42b2dae50da629de0ce.png)\n\n \n\nNotice that the slopes of the slope field are horizontal along the lines $x = -2$ and $y = 2,$ as shown above. Therefore, the right-hand side of the differential equation should evaluate to zero on both of these lines. \n\nSo, our differential equation must be\n\n$$\ny' = (x+2)(y-2).\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $a = -2,$ $b = 2,$ and $a + b=0.$" 13194139534987 true] [17592186171820 1145 "![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3350/Source/Images/3327d1834e198b8eab50a19a54e5b9b1.png)\n\nNotice that the slopes of the slope field are horizontal along the lines $x = -2$ and $y = 2$, as shown above. Therefore, the right-hand side of the differential equation should evaluate to zero on both of these lines.\n\nSo, our differential equation must be\n\n$$\ny' = (x+2)(y-2)\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $a = -2$, $b = 2$, and\n\n$$\na + b=0\n$$" 13194139534987 false] [17592186171947 1020 #edb/ref 17592186325160 13194139534987 true] [17592186171947 1020 #edb/ref 17592186325167 13194139534987 true] [17592186171947 1020 #edb/ref 17592186325174 13194139534987 true] [17592186171947 1020 #edb/ref 17592186325181 13194139534987 true] [17592186171948 1020 #edb/ref 17592186325083 13194139534987 true] [17592186171948 1020 #edb/ref 17592186325111 13194139534987 true] [17592186171948 1020 #edb/ref 17592186325118 13194139534987 true] [17592186171948 1020 #edb/ref 17592186325125 13194139534987 true] [17592186171948 1020 #edb/ref 17592186325153 13194139534987 true] [17592186171949 1020 #edb/ref 17592186325076 13194139534987 true] [17592186171949 1020 #edb/ref 17592186325097 13194139534987 true] [17592186171949 1020 #edb/ref 17592186325104 13194139534987 true] [17592186172014 1020 #edb/ref 17592186325090 13194139534987 true] [17592186172014 1020 #edb/ref 17592186325132 13194139534987 true] [17592186172014 1020 #edb/ref 17592186325139 13194139534987 true] [17592186172014 1020 #edb/ref 17592186325146 13194139534987 true] [17592186172492 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139534987 true] [17592186172492 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14045300/assets/423d393e3ec1f666f384da26ce1df67fd38b977ccad6ff1f56360346ed36d0cc.png)\n\nIdentify the differential equation that generated the slope field above." 13194139534987 true] [17592186172492 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3350/Source/Images/q-49940.png)\n\nIdentify the differential equation that generated the slope field above." 13194139534987 false] [17592186172492 1145 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14045300/assets/423d393e3ec1f666f384da26ce1df67fd38b977ccad6ff1f56360346ed36d0cc.png)\n\n \n\nNotice the following properties of the given slope field:\n\n- First, the slope lines on the same vertical line are all the same. This means that the slope does not vary with $y.$ In other words, ${y}^{′}$ is a function of $x$ only.\n\nOf the given equations, the only equations that satisfy this condition are the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & {y}^{′}=\\frac{1}{1+{x}^{2}} \\\\  & {y}^{′}={e}^{x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Second, the slopes become horizontal as $x\\to \\pm \\infty .$ \n\nOf the remaining options, the only one that gives this behavior is\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=\\frac{1}{1+{x}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nNotice that\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=\\frac{1}{1+{x}^{2}}\\to 0\\,\\text{as}\\,x\\to \\pm \\infty .\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slopes of the slope field for the differential equation ${y}^{′}=\\frac{1}{1+{x}^{2}}$ become horizontal as $x\\to \\pm \\infty .$\nBy contrast, \n\n$$\n{y}^{′}={e}^{x}\\to \\infty \\,\\text{as}\\,x\\to \\infty .\n$$\n\nTherefore, the required equation is\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=\\frac{1}{1+{x}^{2}}.\n$$" 13194139534987 true] [17592186172494 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139534987 true] [17592186172494 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14045300/assets/fb373420ddaa81aa41e85145dce11d71068740e8039522c5d68d861de606bec3.png)\n\nThe differential equation that generates the slope field shown above is given by\n${y}^{′}=(x-a)(y-b).$\nFind the value of $a+b.$" 13194139534987 true] [17592186172494 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3350/Source/Images/q-50108.png)\n\nThe differential equation that generates the slope field shown above is given by\n$y^{′} = (x - a)(y - b)$.\nFind the value of $a + b$." 13194139534987 false] [17592186172494 1145 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14045300/assets/e9011b88061abae826e12742ccd47a272bef9e969f204cd7e8ca63edf908a7f8.png)\n\n \n\nNotice that the slopes of the slope field are horizontal along the lines $x=2$ and $y=2,$ as shown above. Therefore, the right-hand side of the differential equation should evaluate to zero on both of these lines. \n\nSo, our differential equation must be\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=(x-2)(y-2).\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $a=2,$ $b=2,$ and $a+b=4.$" 13194139534987 true] [17592186172495 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139534987 true] [17592186172495 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14045300/assets/50e6b2cca3b08d4b98d1a7be174816cd8e3d726b4e9af29e6605bf365a474eb3.png)\n\nThe diagram shown above is the slope field for which of the following differential equations?" 13194139534987 true] [17592186172495 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3350/Source/Images/q-50160.png)\n\nThe diagram shown above is the slope field for which of the following differential equations?" 13194139534987 false] [17592186172495 1145 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14045300/assets/0b09389629bdd17043a57b2e39551543475e2d19772b41d7b74d3293f4c91aa6.png)\n\n \n\nNotice the following properties of the given slope field:\n\n- First, the slopes of the slope field are horizontal along the lines $x=2$ and $y=2,$ as shown above. Therefore, the right-hand side of the differential equation should evaluate to zero on both of these lines. \n\nOf the given equations, the only equations that satisfy this condition are the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =(x-2)(y-2)^{2} \\\\ {y}^{′} & =(x-2)(y-2)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Second, the lines $x=2$ and $y=2$ divide the plane into $4$ regions. The sign of the slopes in each of the $4$ regions are easily deduced by considering the behavior of the solution curves in these regions. This information is summarized in the following table:\n\n| $y>2$ | $↘$\n\n$(-)$ | $↗$\n\n$(+)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $y<2$ | $↘$\n\n$(-)$ | $↗$\n\n$(+)$ |\